Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::istringstream stream(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(stream, line)) {
        html += line + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text as input and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to the `html` string.

Here's an example usage:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, world!\nThis is a test.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Output:

```
<h1>Hello, world!</h1>
<p>This is a test.</p>
```

Note that this is a simple implementation and may not handle edge cases or syntax errors in the markdown text.

